.TH EUCLID-CLI-ENS-RESEND-MESSAGES 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.73" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-ens-resend-messages \- hand a topic's stored messages to its subscribers again
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli ens resend-messages
.BR \-\-topic | \-t
.I NAME
[\fB\-\-message\-id\fR|\fB\-m\fR \fIID\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Delivers what a topic still holds to its subscriptions again, oldest
first, each message with the payload, attributes and priority it was
published with.
.SH WHAT IT IS FOR
A topic is not consumed the way a queue is. Publishing fans a message out
to the subscriptions there and then, and what stays behind is the record
of what was published \- kept for the topic's retention period, and once
a subscriber has consumed the queue message it received, that record is
the only copy left.
.PP
So there was no way back to it. A subscriber that was down while the
topic was busy, one subscribed after the fact, or one that acknowledged a
message and then failed to process it had lost those messages for good
even though the topic still had them. This is the way back.
.SH IT GOES TO EVERY SUBSCRIBER
Not only the one that missed something. A resend is a publish of what was
already published: every subscription on the topic receives every message
it covers, again, and a subscriber that processed them the first time
will see them a second.
.PP
Whether that matters is the subscriber's business \- a consumer that is
idempotent does not care, and one that is not will double-process. On a
busy topic, narrow it with
.B \-\-message\-id
rather than replaying a fortnight of traffic to everybody.
.PP
The messages keep their own ids, so a subscriber that records what it has
seen can tell a resend from a new publish. The queue message each one
becomes is new and has an id of its own, which is why a resend is
delivered at all rather than being taken for a duplicate and dropped.
.SH HELD MESSAGES ARE NOT RESENT
A message published while the topic was stopped is
.I held
\- it has never been delivered at all, and
.BR euclid-cli-ens-start-topic (1)
is what releases it and marks it delivered afterwards. Handing one over
from here would deliver it without marking it, and the next
.B start-topic
would deliver it a second time.
.PP
They are counted instead. A non-zero
.I held
in the answer means there is a backlog that
.B start-topic
should release.
.PP
A stopped topic is refused outright, for the same reason: it delivers
nothing by somebody's decision, and this would be the one way around
that.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-topic " " \fINAME\fR
Topic name. A full ERN also works, and is what reaches another namespace.
Required.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-message\-id " " \fIID\fR
Resend only this message, as
.BR euclid-cli-ens-list-messages (1)
reports its id. The message must belong to the named topic; one that
belongs to another is refused rather than fanned out to subscriptions it
was never published to.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
A summary
.RI ( "{ern, resent, held}" )
is printed as JSON.
.I resent
is how many went to the subscriptions;
.I held
how many were passed over as never having been delivered.
.TP
.B 1
No topic of that name, the topic is stopped, no message of that id, that
message belongs to another topic, or the caller was not granted the
topic.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
# Everything the topic still holds
euclid-cli ens resend-messages --topic orders

# One message, which is what a busy topic usually wants
euclid-cli ens resend-messages --topic orders \e
    --message-id 7c1f2f5e-3a21-4e6b-9a0c-5f2b9e1d4c33

# Find the one to resend first
euclid-cli ens list-messages --topic orders
.fi
.RE
